# Summary
Introduced/incomplete MFA enforcement: privileged MFA was added as a new security boundary, but enforcement was not wired into server-side request handling. The new frontend block is bypassable because the backend proxy and Core API routers do not enforce the new dependency.
The new `require_mfa_for_privileged` dependency correctly returns 403 for privileged Auth0 users without MFA evidence, but it is not imported or attached to any production router. The existing privileged routers still depend only on `authenticate_request` plus role checks, and the admin BFF proxy forwards requests to the Core API for any active admin session without checking `_session_mfa_required`. As a result, the React overlay only blocks normal UI navigation; a user who can authenticate as an admin/owner/platform owner without completing MFA can still call `/admin/api/core/...` from the same browser session, or call the Core API endpoints directly with an access token, and reach tenant admin, tenant signup, and platform admin operations. The tests only exercise the dependency in isolation and do not verify that it is wired into the route stack.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm require_mfa_for_privileged itself blocks privileged no-MFA Auth0 sessions when invoked.
- [x] Confirm production Core API privileged routers omit Depends(require_mfa_for_privileged).
- [x] Confirm a no-MFA privileged Auth0-style identity reaches a privileged Core API route through the actual FastAPI dependency stack.
- [x] Confirm the admin session layer detects mfa_required but the /admin/api/core proxy does not enforce it server-side.
- [x] Confirm the frontend MFA block is client-side only and therefore not a reliable security boundary.
## Report
Validated. This is a Python/FastAPI authz wiring flaw, so crash/valgrind are not applicable; I used targeted code inspection plus FastAPI TestClient PoCs that exercise the real route stack. Evidence: app/core/security.py:258-281 defines require_mfa_for_privileged and it correctly raises 403 when Auth0 is active and a privileged user has mfa_verified=False. However app/api/v1/routes.py:40 imports authenticate_request, require_min_role, require_platform_owner, require_service, but not require_mfa_for_privileged. The privileged routers at app/api/v1/routes.py:248-263 depend only on authenticate_request plus role checks, and they are included directly into the live /v1 router at app/api/v1/routes.py:3214-3220. A targeted grep found require_mfa_for_privileged only in app/core/security.py and tests, not production route wiring. Runtime PoC #1 patched token decoding to simulate an Auth0 admin token with no amr MFA evidence. Directly invoking the intended gate produced EXPECTED_MFA_GATE 403 MFA is required for privileged roles, but the real /v1/me/tenants route returned 200 and reached the fake DB dependency, proving the live route stack allowed the same no-MFA admin identity. Output: AUTH_STATE user ['admin'] mfa_verified=False; EXPECTED_MFA_GATE 403 ...; FAKE_DB_FETCH_REACHED; CORE_ROUTE_STATUS 200. For the admin BFF, app/admin/routes.py:389-409 reports mfa_required, but app/admin/routes.py:503-521 only checks _active_session and then forwards to the Core API. Runtime PoC #2 inserted an active admin session with mfa_verified=False. /admin/api/session returned mfa_required: True, then /admin/api/core/v1/me/tenants still reached the mocked upstream with Bearer access-token-no-mfa and returned 200. This confirms the React-only overlay at admin-panel/src/components/layout/AdminLayout.jsx:73-131 is bypassable by direct backend/proxy requests.

